    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a deck project in vancouver and need to double check some math. we're using western red cedar for the framing. can you explain the difference between live load and dead load calculations for the deck structure and how they factor into the beam sizing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In deck design, live load refers to the weight of people, furniture, and other movingObjects that will be on the deck, typically ranging from 40-100 kg/m² (80-200 lbs/ft²). Dead load, on the other hand, is the weight of the deck itself, including the decking boards, joists, beams, and anchors. To size beams, you would add live load to dead load: consult the Canadian Building Code (IBC) or a deck design software like ETAP, for specific load values and calculation methods to determine beam sizes.",
      "source": "target",
